# packs/companion/extension/stewards_companion.control
#
# stewards_companion — the companion pack (forge.sql + companion.sql +
# steward-tools.sql) packaged as a real Postgres extension. Proof subject
# for D2A (#319): pure SQL, no pgrx, no native code. See the battle plan:
# .spec/proposals/d2a-pack-extension-battle-plan.md
#
# Two schemas (forge, companion) are created BY THE SCRIPT itself — no
# `schema =` line here on purpose (mechanic #2 of the battle plan: a
# single-schema `schema =` directive can't express that).
comment = 'stewards_companion: voice companion pack (forge, reminders, bell, steward-tools) for pg_ai_stewards'
default_version = '0.2.0'
relocatable = false
requires = 'pg_ai_stewards'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# DROP-survivors posture — RATIFIED (battle plan mechanic #3).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Objects the script CREATEs (schemas forge + companion, the reminders and
# forged_tools tables, every companion.* / forge.forge_register function)
# are extension members: DROP EXTENSION drops them. But several things this
# pack touches are DATA, not owned objects, and need a deliberate posture.
# The uninstall protocol is a documented ORDER — call the helper, THEN drop:
#
#   SELECT companion.companion_uninstall();   -- the documented step
#   DROP EXTENSION stewards_companion;         -- (add CASCADE to also take
#                                              --  forged tools; see below)
#
# 1. tool_defs rows (this pack's own: forge_register, reminder_set/list/
#    cancel, companion_bell, companion_approve, and — at 0.2.0 — forge_start,
#    work_item_unstick, model_health, models_health_check) are INSERTs, not
#    owned objects. companion_uninstall() DEACTIVATES them (active=false) —
#    never DELETEs. A re-install's ON CONFLICT upsert flips active back true,
#    so history and any references survive an uninstall/reinstall cycle.
#
# 2. Forged tools (the runtime-created forge.<name> functions and their
#    forge.forged_tools rows) are NOT extension members — forge_register
#    made them at runtime, outside CREATE EXTENSION. So a plain
#    `DROP EXTENSION stewards_companion` REFUSES while any forged function
#    lives in the forge schema (Postgres won't drop a non-empty schema it
#    owns without CASCADE). That refusal is a FEATURE: it protects operator-
#    forged work from a careless drop. `DROP EXTENSION ... CASCADE` is the
#    explicit, eyes-open choice to destroy the forged functions too.
#
# 3. The forge pipelines row (family='forge') and the companion intent row
#    (slug='companion') are KEPT on uninstall — ledger history, receipts,
#    and work_items reference them. companion_uninstall() does not touch
#    them; DROP EXTENSION does not either (they are rows, not members).
#
# 4. stewards.config 'arc_c_dynamic_write_allowlist' — DROP EXTENSION does
#    NOT shrink it back; the widened allowlist is security-relevant.
#    companion_uninstall() SHRINKS it, removing only THIS pack's write-tool
#    names (leaving any operator or other-pack additions intact). This is a
#    documented manual step, deliberately NOT an event trigger: the drop is
#    a rare, operator-driven act, and a sql_drop event trigger firing helper
#    logic mid-DROP is more surprise than safety.
#
# 5. companion.reminders is a member TABLE: DROP EXTENSION drops it (and its
#    rows) like any owned object. pg_extension_config_dump (see the
#    PACKAGING footer in the --X.sql scripts) marks its CONTENTS so a
#    pg_dump/restore CARRIES the rows across a copy — it does NOT make the
#    table outlive its own DROP. Understood and asserted: stage 5 proves
#    dump/restore survival; stage 6 proves DROP takes the table.
#
# Every clause above is an executable assertion in test-extension.sh
# (stages 5-6). companion_uninstall() itself is installed by the PACKAGING
# footer of the --0.1.0.sql / --0.2.0.sql scripts (and replaced, widened to
# the 0.2.0 tool set, by the --0.1.0--0.2.0.sql delta).
